1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
So today, in a little bit, we're going to be talking about older Scouts.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
I have a story that I think is really funny.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
A number of years ago I had a couple of older Scouts who were fascinated with the television show

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Beavis and Butthead.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
If you're not familiar with Beavis and Butthead, don't waste your time.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
It was kind of a cool show, okay,

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:36,000
But they just embodied being Beavis and Butthead 24 hours a day, seven days a week, it seemed,

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,000
And you know, on a weekend camping trip, and then another weekend camping trip

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
and then a week at summer camp. that can get pretty old.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
I stumbled on a way of getting them to stop without asking them to,

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
And that was when myself and another one of the adult leaders within their hearing

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
started repeating all of the things that they were imitating,

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
and not doing it in a cynical or mean way or anything like that,

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
just doing it and enjoying it along the same lines that they did.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
And I can remember the look that I got from one of them when they discovered that we were doing this.

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
And you know what,

18
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Once they discovered that we were enjoying that and having a lot of fun with it,

19
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
they never did it again.

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
It worked like a charm. It was amazing.

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Hey, this is podcast number 370..

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast. This is Clark Green.

23
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
A very brief few words of introduction today.

24
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Last week's podcast about patrols and planning, where I answered email questions,

25
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
received several very nice comments over on Facebook.

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Thank you, Larry Jennings, Pete Parris and John Mello.

27
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Thanks also to Heath, who got in touch through the website.

28
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Thank you for your kind words

29
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
And thank you to everyone else who has been in touch via email this week.

30
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
You can get in touch with me at Clark at scoutmastercgcom.

31
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
I'll tell you more about that when we get towards the end of the podcast,

32
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
But today we're going to be talking about older Scouts.

33
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
So let's get started, shall we?

34
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Let's talk about older Scouts

35
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Now. to begin with, when I'm talking about older Scouts,

36
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
I am talking about Scouts that have left intermediate or middle school

37
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
and moved into high school.

38
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Most of the time, that means they're around 14 years of age,

39
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
One of the first things to notice. in the United States,

40
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
our children move from elementary school to a middle or intermediate school

41
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
And then from that school into high school.

42
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
So you got to ask yourself what's going on there.

43
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Why is that happening.

44
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
There are developmental things going on in young people's lives

45
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
that make this a good idea.

46
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Now, good, bad or indifferent whether you like the way that this is done

47
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
or not, this is the way it happens.

48
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Now, if we go and look at the Scouting program that the BSA offers,

49
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
we have Scouts from 11 to the age of 18..

50
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:54,000
In a Scout Troop, which is really unusual if you compare it with the rest of the world of Scouting,

51
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Because the last time I checked the oldest age that a young person could remain

52
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
in a Scout Troop as a Scout was in Germany and that was the age of 16.

53
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
And there was some kind of special circumstance there.

54
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
That we keep Scouts in a Troop until they reach their 18th birthday

55
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
is an anomaly. It's kind of unusual.

56
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
So in the BSA we have this interesting conundrum to work with.

57
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
We have younger Scouts who are 10 and a half, 11,, 12, 13 years old,

58
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
and we have older Scouts who are 14,, 15,, 16,, 17 years old.

59
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
What happens a lot of times is you volunteer, you become a Scout Master

60
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
and there may be older Scouts involved with the Troop.

61
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
There may not, but at some point you understand

62
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
you've got some young people who are very different developmentally

63
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
and have a different set of concerns and interests than your younger Scouts,

64
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
And this sets up several things that can be a conflict.

65
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
It sets up things that can be problems, and I hear about them pretty regularly.

66
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And then we act as though we're the first people to discover this thing

67
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
and we've got to come up with a solution for it, because our older Scouts

68
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
don't seem to be as interested and engaged as they used to be.

69
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
I'll give you a really good example of this.

70
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
When I directed our summer camp and served on our summer camp staff,

71
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,000
there was always some kind of drive to create an older Scout program,

72
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Thinking that the reason that these Scouts aren't coming back

73
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,000
is because they've already done most of the stuff that we offer in a program.

74
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
so we should have some cool stuff just for the older Scouts to do.

75
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
And that makes a lot of sense, doesn't it?

76
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
But you know what? It basically never put a dent in the numbers,

77
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
because amping up the wow factor of the program

78
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
or trying to appeal to older Scouts with special activities and things like that

79
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
is what I call the bread and circuses approach,

80
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And if you're not familiar with the metaphor,

81
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
bread and circuses means that you're placating real concerns

82
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
with superficial means of appeasement and diversions and distractions,

83
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
without really getting at the heart of the matter.

84
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
So let's talk about the heart of the matter.

85
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
If simply turning up the wow factor doesn't keep older Scouts engaged,

86
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
what really keeps them interested and engaged, what keeps them around?

87
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
So before we try to answer that question,

88
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
let's really observe the situation that we find ourselves in

89
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
when it comes to working with older Scouts

90
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
And let's ask ourselves what do they want to get out of the program?

91
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
They want to become Eagle Scouts as one standard response.

92
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
They're kind of in it for getting the Eagle and all that that involves.

93
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and some Scouts become discouraged by that drive

94
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
because it seems like it's a means to an end

95
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
and they just want to get it over with.

96
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
And as positive as that drive can be for them,

97
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
it can also lead to some cynicism about being involved in everything

98
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
and I'm just kind of holding on because I want to become an Eagle Scout.

99
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
So let's set that whole part of it aside.

100
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Let's realize that that is a thing, but it's not the thing.

101
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:11,000
So what do these Scouts, these 14 to 17 year old Scouts,

102
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
what are they looking for and what do they really want?

103
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:18,000
In the simplest of terms, I think they want what everybody wants

104
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
and that is kind of to hang out with their friends

105
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
and associate with people their own age,

106
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
You know. as I mentioned, they want to pay attention

107
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
to their own advancement and progress.

108
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,000
They want to have increasing levels of independence and freedom

109
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
to discover and explore without people looking over their shoulder,

110
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
and they have this desire to be considered an adult.

111
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,000
They're in those years where childhood is being left behind

112
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
and they start looking forward to life as an adult

113
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
and they're pushed and pulled in many different ways

114
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
and, like everybody else on the face of the planet,

115
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
they have a desire to be useful and appreciated.

116
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Now let's look at what we Scouters want from our older Scouts

117
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
As far as association goes. we think it's really really important

118
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
that they associate with younger Scouts

119
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
and we come up with all kinds of schemes to make that happen.

120
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
With older Scouts we're happy to have them pay attention

121
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
to their own individual progress and advancement,

122
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
but we really want them to pay attention

123
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
to younger Scouts advancement and progress.

124
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:23,000
In those two things we're kind of working counter

125
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
a little bit to what they want.

126
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
We have a set of expectations that they may not share

127
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,000
and then, where the expectations align a little bit more

128
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
is we want them to develop a sense of responsibility

129
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
for others as well as themselves.

130
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
We want them to return what they have received

131
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
in positions of responsibility

132
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
and we want to begin to trust and count on them

133
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
as we would an adult.

134
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Some of the expectations an older Scout has

135
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
and a Scouter has are kind of in alignment

136
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and some of them are a little bit opposite,

137
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Especially in the idea that we want them to associate

138
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
with younger Scouts on an ongoing basis

139
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
and take responsibility for them

140
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
and their advancement and things like that.

141
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
That's normally where kind of the point of friction comes in.

142
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Next, I would like to point out that you should always be having

143
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
a challenge working with older Scouts.

144
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
You're always going to have that challenge.

145
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
It's never going to be solved

146
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
and your results are going to be all over the map.

147
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
and that's fine, because that's the way that this works.

148
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
Give it a little bit of time,

149
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
a little bit of experience and practice.

150
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,000
You will get better at it.

151
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
But we are dealing with people

152
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,000
in a particularly mercurial time of their lives, right,

153
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
Where they are looking for identities

154
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:34,000
and they're seeking things out,

155
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
trying to figure out what they're going to do.

156
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
They're pushed and pulled in all kinds of directions

157
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:39,000
by all kinds of forces

158
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
and we can choose to be one of those pushers and pullers

159
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,000
or we can choose to be a bit of a refuge

160
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
from all of those pressures.

161
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
So to become more successful

162
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:50,000
in working with older Scouts,

163
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
we need to recognize that most of us have a few blind spots.

164
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
One of the first big blind spots is we have a program

165
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and we present the program

166
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
like we present a well-prepared dinner, you know,

167
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,000
and we lay it out there

168
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
and we're very disappointed

169
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
when our Scouts turn their nose up at it.

170
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
And if that's happening on a regular basis,

171
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
we've got to stop driving the program.

172
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
rather than working with individual Scouts,

173
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:16,000
We have to balance the interest of the individual

174
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
against the interest of the group,

175
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:22,000
and that is a process that never stops

176
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
and it's the way that we occupy a lot of our time as Scouts.

177
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
One of the other things we tend to do

178
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
is we complicate the simple and simplify the complex.

179
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:35,000
So when you're dealing with a challenge

180
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
with older Scouts- and younger Scouts too,

181
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
you know the most obvious answer

182
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,000
is probably the right answer,

183
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
but not necessarily 100% of the time,

184
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
When we listen carefully to what individual Scouts are telling us.

185
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
we will be better armed to divine

186
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
between the complex and the simple.

187
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
The way Scouts behave, older or younger,

188
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
is an expression of a number of complex causes and motivations.

189
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
They will act in ways that are a little confusing.

190
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
They do silly, stupid things on occasion

191
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
because they are working out how you become an adult

192
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
and they're not necessarily interested in listening

193
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
to what you think about how they ought to become an adult.

194
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
You were not very interested in listening

195
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
to the adults around you during this time of your life.

196
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,000
I would guess

197
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
You were much more interested in finding your own way

198
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:30,000
and you made a number of mistakes

199
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and you made a number of bad choices.

200
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Who do you need to be

201
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
in response to what is going on in their lives?

202
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
Well, the last thing you need to be is their friend.

203
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
Now I have enjoyed a pretty good friendship

204
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
with a lot of my older Scouts,

205
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
but that friendship has to kind of be at arm's length,

206
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
because I have more important work to do for them

207
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
and I have more of a responsibility for them

208
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
than just being their pal.

209
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
I also have to be an authority figure at a time in their lives

210
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
where they're liable to make some bad choices

211
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
and I have to have the authority to deal with those things,

212
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
and friendship can kind of dilute that authority.

213
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
There is a razor's edge of a balance

214
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
When I'm talking about having some authority

215
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
and everything like that.

216
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
that doesn't mean you're unapproachable,

217
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
you're unpleasant, you're gruff, you're mean.

218
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
It means that you accept the idea

219
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
that you are going to make unpopular decisions

220
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
or take unpopular actions every once in a while

221
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
and you don't let it keep you up at night,

222
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
because this is your role.

223
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
You're an adult, they are children,

224
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Your sense of responsibility and judgment

225
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
is a little more honed and attuned

226
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
and you have the benefit of more experience than they do,

227
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
and sometimes you will have to impose that upon them

228
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
In response to who they are and their time of life.

229
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
what else do we need to be?

230
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Well, we need to be very good observers and listeners

231
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:57,000
and in my experience, you cannot simultaneously listen to

232
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
or observe what other people are doing

233
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and judge them at the same time.

234
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
So there needs to be a part of your attention

235
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
as an observer and as a listener

236
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
that is free of coming to judgment

237
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:12,000
as you observe and listen.

238
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
Do you understand what I'm saying?

239
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,000
You have to occupy a much more neutral territory

240
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
before you decide that you're going to make

241
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:25,000
some kind of a judgment about the propriety

242
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
or the advisability of a certain course of action.

243
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and this is why it's good

244
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
to have a little bit of a remove

245
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
from being invested in winning the friendship of older Scouts,

246
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
because you need to exercise a whole lot of patience

247
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
and if we allow our emotions to take over

248
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and we feel slighted or disrespected,

249
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
then we're going to run into problems.

250
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
So the basic answer to the question

251
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
of who do you need to be

252
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
when it comes to working with older Scouts

253
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
and responding to what they need

254
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
is: you need to be an adult,

255
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
You need to be kind, courteous,

256
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
considerate, loyal, fair

257
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
and you need to have some empathy.

258
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
You need to be approachable and friendly, of course,

259
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
but you need to differentiate between the idea

260
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
of the responsibilities you have for young people

261
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
and the idea that you're going to be their friend,

262
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
So seeing misbehavior or problems

263
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
as a personal affront

264
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
from somebody who's supposed to be your friend,

265
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:29,000
rather than just the general process

266
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
that young people are going through-

267
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
making poor choices,

268
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
trying things out-

269
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
your sense of fairness

270
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
is going to come from objectivity,

271
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
and our own desire to be liked

272
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
and appreciated and respected

273
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
will sometimes prevent us

274
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
from being what we need to be,

275
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
and that is decisive and directional.

276
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
Now, when it comes to working with older Scouts

277
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
in positions of responsibility,

278
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
I'm going to reiterate

279
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
my often made plea

280
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
to not become their boss

281
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
or their drill sergeant

282
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
or their teacher,

283
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
but that you are a partner with them

284
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
in this work.

285
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
and while their position of responsibility

286
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
has some practical outcome,

287
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
we need to also realize

288
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
it's really part of the game,

289
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
because it's their opportunity

290
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
to learn skills and to develop character

291
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
that's going to help them

292
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
for the rest of their lives.

293
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
Now, if you think you can teach them

294
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
or think you can imbue them with character,

295
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
you're going to be disappointed,

296
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
because the nature of people

297
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
at this age

298
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
is to try things out, to experiment

299
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
and to do things for themselves.

300
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
so partner with them

301
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
as they go through this evolution

302
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
of discovery and talk to them about it,

303
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
not substituting your judgment

304
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
for theirs,

305
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
but asking them how they've judged

306
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
a given situation

307
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
or how they see the outcome of a certain action.

308
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
and I've found over time

309
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
this is the most fascinating,

310
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
the most satisfying aspect of my work

311
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
as a scouter

312
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
is to be able to have a dialogue with them

313
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
about what they're thinking,

314
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
the decision making processes

315
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
they're going through

316
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
and how they're judging different situations.

317
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
I can't do that

318
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
if I'm going to be their boss,

319
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
their drill sergeant or their teacher.

320
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
I am much more interested

321
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
in their judgment,

322
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
in their actions, than I am

323
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
telling them how they should think,

324
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
how they should feel,

325
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
how they should judge given situations.

326
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
if you open up a dialogue

327
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
about those things,

328
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
you'll get to express your opinion at some point.

329
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
but your opinion isn't the most important part of this.

330
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
discovering what they're up to

331
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
and what they're thinking is much more important.

332
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
while we're on the subject

333
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
of working with older scouts,

334
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
let's talk about the dark side of the moon,

335
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
and no, I'm not talking about

336
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
Pink Floyd,

337
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
although you might want to revisit that.

338
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
if you haven't done that in a while,

339
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
it'll put you back into that mindset

340
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
a little bit.

341
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
if you remember the NASA lunar missions,

342
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
there was a point which the spacecraft

343
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
was on the dark side of the moon

344
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
and out of radio contact with Earth

345
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
and, if I remember correctly,

346
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
it lasted about 30 minutes or so

347
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and I can recall this being broadcast on television,

348
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
everybody kind of biting their nails for 30 minutes,

349
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
desperate to hear back from

350
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
the spacecraft.

351
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
while working with younger people

352
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
having a son or a daughter.

353
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
there's going to be at least some period

354
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
of their lives where they are on the dark side

355
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
of the moon. they're out of radio contact.

356
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
you really can't tell what's going on in there,

357
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
just like the NASA missions.

358
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
you're kind of biting your nails, wondering

359
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
if you're going to get back in contact.

360
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
you will.

361
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
you won't be able to see what's going on,

362
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
you won't be able to hear what's going on

363
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
and very little information is going to be shared with you.

364
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
but you will

365
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
get back into contact.

366
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
another good thing to talk about is

367
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
the way that most young people need to express

368
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
their own rugged individualism.

369
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
now, it's kind of interesting

370
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
that over 30 years of being a scoutmaster,

371
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
working with scouts, that that

372
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
rugged individualism is really

373
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
not very individual

374
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
at all. it's pretty predictable.

375
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
you know a certain kind of way

376
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
of wearing your clothes

377
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
or doing your hair. patterns of

378
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
speech and attitudes

379
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
come up and everybody

380
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
who wants to be a rugged individual

381
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
follows those particular paradigms

382
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
right. it's kind of amusing

383
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
to watch that happen. and think back

384
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
for a moment. when you were that age,

385
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
what things were you doing

386
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
that were driving your parents right up a wall?

387
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
was it what you were wearing,

388
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
the way you kept your

389
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
hair, the things that you listen to,

390
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
the things that you read,

391
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
the general attitude that you assumed

392
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
with them. it's very likely

393
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
that you did something

394
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and now take that experience

395
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
and build it into your experience of working with older

396
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
scouts. they're going to do something

397
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
that's just going to drive you crazy

398
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
and if you want it to be repeated often,

399
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
let them know that you don't like it.

400
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
some things are worth going to the mat over

401
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
and battling for, and these things

402
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
are always big things.

403
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
save your energy for the big things

404
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
and let the little things alone.

405
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
sometimes I've had older scouts

406
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
go up with purple hair

407
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
or they fall in love

408
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
with a hat or some element

409
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
of costume

410
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
that they have to wear 24 hours

411
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
a day, 7 days a week.

412
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
a lot of times they will fall out of love

413
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
with their scout uniform

414
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
or they will take a cynical approach to something

415
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
I consider to be sacrosanct and sacred.

416
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
well, I'm going to save

417
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
my powder for things that

418
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
really matter and do my best

419
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
to do nothing but

420
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
raise an eyebrow at the other things.

421
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and the uniform thing is something to talk about

422
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
for a moment, because

423
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
I think it drives a lot of people crazy.

424
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
I know it drove me crazy. a lot of times

425
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
my older scouts would show up no uniform

426
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
or their uniform would be very sloppy

427
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
and kind of slapdash,

428
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
and I wrestled and fought with that for a while

429
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
and then I just kind of decided

430
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
that I had more important things

431
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
to do than to worry about this

432
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
and so stopped paying

433
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
all that much attention to it.

434
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
that might raise your eyebrows.

435
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
I would just encourage you to put your energy

436
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
where it really matters,

437
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
and that's the development of character

438
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
and assuring everyone's safety and well being.

439
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
the other thing that I believe is important

440
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
is not fighting against the idea.

441
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
older scouts want to hang out together

442
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
and are not necessarily interested

443
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
in just kind of hanging out

444
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
and spending time with scouts who are several years younger.

445
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
now just like uniforms.

446
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
if you want to pay attention to that

447
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
and you want to spend a whole lot of energy on that,

448
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
then you can advise against it.

449
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
in last week's podcast I was dealing with

450
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
a question that came from

451
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
there was a problem with patrols

452
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
because all the older scouts would leave

453
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
their patrol assignments and just go hang out together.

454
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
well, that means that they ought to be in their same patrol.

455
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
you know the answer that comes back to that is:

456
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
well, if they're not in patrols with younger scouts?

457
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
what will the younger scouts do?

458
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
and my answer is: the younger scouts will be fine,

459
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
they'll be in a patrol,

460
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
they'll elect their patrol leader

461
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
and then the older scouts will help them

462
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
find to being in their patrol

463
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
on a permanent basis.

464
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
because if you're looking at it from

465
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
a purely logistical and management perspective,

466
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
it makes perfect sense.

467
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
but when it comes to scouting,

468
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
it doesn't necessarily make a whole lot of sense

469
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
because you're not managing employees,

470
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
you're dealing with people at a very murcury age

471
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
and you are working against

472
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
what is happening in the rest of their lives.

473
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
well, I've got to say,

474
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
have absolutely zero problem

475
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
with getting older scouts to work with younger scouts

476
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
to instruct them,

477
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
to help them with their advancement,

478
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
to help them on camping trips and backpacking trips.

479
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
it's not a problem.

480
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
it would be much more of a problem

481
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
if I was to require them

482
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
to be in the same patrol with those younger scouts.

483
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
let's break this down by reviewing

484
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
what we've been talking about.

485
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
if you are running into challenges

486
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
and difficulties working with older scouts

487
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
or you have a sense that

488
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
they're not really buying into what's going on

489
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
and you can't trust them or depend on them,

490
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
I would not be surprised that that's happening,

491
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
because that is what is

492
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
generally going to happen.

493
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
don't be dismayed. you have lots of company.

494
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
if you'd like to smooth out these challenges

495
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
and difficulties, then

496
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
consider what older scouts want.

497
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
consider how that

498
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
may correspond with what you want.

499
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
consider who you need to be

500
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
in response to who they are

501
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
in their lives at the moment,

502
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
and begin watching

503
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
and listening and see what needs to change.

504
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
if you will

505
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
take my advice and remain

506
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
eternally optimistic-

507
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
always see that glasses half full

508
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
and look for

509
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
positive things

510
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
rather than negative things-

511
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
the whole thing will begin to get better,

512
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
because there's a lot of positive there.

513
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
spend your energy

514
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
on looking for that

515
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
and cultivating that.

516
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
if you spend your energy resisting

517
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
things that you don't like,

518
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
to build on the things that you do like.

519
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
so I hope that's helpful.

520
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
I'd love to hear your thoughts on this.

521
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
you can get in touch with me pretty easily

522
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
and I'm going to tell you how to do that in just a moment.

523
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
well, thanks so much for listening to this

524
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
370th edition

525
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
of the scoutmaster podcast.

526
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
keeping in touch is pretty easy.

527
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
you can go to scoutmastercgcom

528
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
where you'll find lots and lots of articles

529
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and lots and lots of podcasts,

